TiddlyFlashcards is configured in two places: the plugin inside TiddlyWiki, and the add-on inside Anki. Do the TiddlyWiki side first, because the add-on has to be told where your TiddlyWiki installation and your wiki live.

!! 1. Before you start

TiddlyFlashcards keeps the flashcards of each tiddler in a separate data tiddler, linked back through a `source` field that holds the tiddler's title. Renaming a tiddler must not break that link, so TiddlyWiki needs to know that `source` contains a title.

Install both plugins:

* [[$:/plugins/flibbles/relink|https://flibbles.github.io/tw5-relink/#%24%3A%2Fplugins%2Fflibbles%2Frelink]]
* [[$:/plugins/flibbles/relink-fieldnames|https://flibbles.github.io/tw5-relink/#%24%3A%2Fplugins%2Fflibbles%2Frelink-fieldnames]]

Then open relink's settings and add `source` to the field names whose values are titles:

```
source: title
```

<<readme-img img:"$:/plugins/uzvg/TiddlyFlashcards/images/RelinkConfigurations.png" caption:"Add source as a field whose value is a title">>

!! 2. Settings in TiddlyWiki

!!! 1. Display Filter

Which tiddlers get the card creator at the bottom. The filter is a list: one tiddler selection per line.

To show the panel only on tiddlers tagged `PermanentNotes` or `LiteratureNotes`:

```
[tag[PermanentNotes]]
[tag[LiteratureNotes]]
```

The card creator and the card preview then appear under those tiddlers.

To keep a single tiddler clean even though it matches the filter, give it a `tf-hide` field set to `1`.

!!! 2. DeckMapping rules

Which Anki deck the exported cards go to. A card takes its deck and its tags from the tiddler it belongs to.

That tiddler is called `sourceTiddler` below. TiddlyFlashcards builds the deck name with this filter:

```
[<sourceTiddler>]
:cascade[[$:/config/TiddlyFlashcards/DeckMappingRules]get[text]]
+[!is[blank]else[TFDefault]]
```

When no rule matches, the cards fall back to the `TFDefault` deck.

!!! 3. TagMapping rules

Which tags the cards inherit from their tiddler. Most people never need this one.

```
[<sourceTiddler>tags[]subfilter<TagMappingRules>]
```

`sourceTiddler` is the owning tiddler; `TagMappingRules` is the filter you typed in the settings page. Left blank, the tags pass through unchanged.

For example, drop tags that only make sense on your notes:

```
[remove[Books]]
```

Or drop several at once:

```
[remove[LiteratureNotes PermanentNotes]]
```

Cards from those tiddlers then reach Anki without those tags.

!!! 4. CardListSize

How many preview cards appear on each page of the card list. Six works well for most people.

For a single tiddler, set its `tf-list-size` field instead.

!!! 5. Relink inside card JSON

Relink updates titles in ordinary wikitext, but the card text lives inside JSON. TiddlyFlashcards adds an `application/json` handler so renaming a tiddler also updates the links in its cards. Two settings control it; both are shadow tiddlers, so create a real tiddler with the same title to override them.

''`$:/config/TiddlyFlashcards/Relink/Filter`'' selects the JSON tiddlers that are checked. The default only covers card data:

```
[prefix[$:/Storage/TiddlyFlashcards/DataTiddlers/]]
```

''`$:/config/TiddlyFlashcards/Relink/Fields`'' selects the strings inside the JSON that are checked. Patterns are matched against the end of the key path, and `*` matches one path segment. The default only covers the cards' own fields:

```
fields/*
```

To check just the front and back, or to include a field name with a space:

```
fields/Front fields/Back
```

```
fields/Front [[fields/Back Extra]]
```

Use `*` to check every string value. The default deliberately leaves `modelName` and `modelVersion` alone, so renaming a tiddler that happens to share a note type's name does not rewrite the card.

Relink builds its reference index the first time it is used, so reload the wiki after changing either setting.

!! 3. Settings in Anki

With the add-on installed, open its config: ''Tools'' ➡ ''Add-ons'' ➡ ''TiddlyFlashcards'' ➡ ''config''.

<<readme-img img:"$:/plugins/uzvg/TiddlyFlashcards/images/TiddlyFlashcardsAnkiSettings.png" caption:"TiddlyFlashcards Anki Settings">>

!!! 1. TiddlyWiki binary

The absolute path to your TiddlyWiki binary. On Unix-like systems, `which tiddlywiki` prints it. Fill it in, then click ''test'' to confirm the path works.

!!! 2. TiddlyWiki plugin path

The value of your `$TIDDLYWIKI_PLUGIN_PATH` environment variable. Some Node.js users keep all their TiddlyWiki plugins in one folder.

See [[Environment Variables on Node.js|https://tiddlywiki.com/#Environment%20Variables%20on%20Node.js]].

!!! 3. Add a TiddlyWiki

TiddlyFlashcards works with two kinds of workspace, `folder` and `html`. Add the one that matches your setup.

''API URL'' is the local port your TiddlyWiki listens on. It is meant for jumping straight to a tiddler, but that feature isn't implemented yet, so leave it empty for now.

!!! 4. Save

Click ''save'' and the Anki side is configured. Next, create your first cards: 👉 <<readme-link index:"3">>.

